---
layout: layouts/page.njk
title: "Menu | The Café"
description: "Breakfast and lunch at The Café in Leavenworth, KS. Biscuits and gravy, omelets, wraps, sandwiches, burgers, salads and daily soup."
ogDescription: "Breakfast and lunch in Leavenworth, KS. Call ahead and we will have it waiting."
pageCss: "/assets/css/menu.css"
---
{#- ===========================================================
    /menu

    Same data as the printed tri-fold (src/_data/menu.json), a different
    job. The tri-fold is read at a table with the food already in front of
    you; this page is read on a phone, usually while deciding what to
    order — so the food comes first, straight under the heading. Hours,
    phone and address are in the site footer, not repeated here. The
    dietary explanation sits below the list, but its filter (once marks are
    confirmed) sits above it, next to the items it hides.

    The dietary marks and their meanings come from menu.json's `diets`
    registry, not from anything hard-coded here or in the script below.
    Add a code there and it appears in the key, in the filter, and in the
    structured data without this file changing.
    =========================================================== #}

{#- The marks come from a shared partial so /menu and the 1080p board
    cannot drift apart on the one thing that must not drift: which code
    means what, and in what order. The gate below is this page's, not the
    partial's — see the comment in the partial for why the tri-fold is
    allowed to render marks that this page is not. -#}
{% from "partials/diet-marks.njk" import dietMarks %}

<section class="section menu-body" aria-labelledby="menu-heading">
    <div class="container">
        <h1 id="menu-heading">Menu</h1>
        <p class="menu-lede">Breakfast and lunch, made to order. Everything is available to go. Call ahead and we&rsquo;ll have it waiting.</p>

        {#- Prices on this page are the old menu's, and some of them are still
            being re-costed (see menu.json's priceReview flag). Saying so up
            here rather than in small print at the foot: somebody who drives
            over expecting $8.00 and pays $9.00 remembers that, and the note
            costs one line. Delete it once the cafe is open and the prices
            have settled. -#}
        <p class="menu-note">Prices may change closer to our opening on {{ site.opening.monthDay }}.</p>

        {%- if site.ordering.url %}
        <p class="menu-order">
            <a href="{{ site.ordering.url }}" class="btn btn-primary">{{ site.ordering.label }} for pickup</a>
        </p>
        {%- endif %}

        {#- The filter's mount point, above the list it filters: tapping a
            chip hides items, and that has to happen in view, not somewhere
            the reader scrolled past. Empty in the HTML; the script at the
            foot builds the buttons, so there is never a dead control. Absent
            entirely while the marks are gated, which is also what stops the
            script building chips — it returns on a missing mount rather than
            needing its own copy of the flag. -#}
        {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed %}
        <div class="diet-filter" data-diet-filter hidden></div>
        {%- endif %}

        <h2 id="menu-list-heading" class="visually-hidden">Full menu</h2>

        {#- Live region for the filter. Empty and silent until the script has
            a result count to announce. -#}
        <p class="menu-result" data-menu-result role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

        <div class="menu-grid">
            {%- for c in menu.categories %}
            <section class="menu-cat" data-menu-cat aria-labelledby="cat-{{ c.id }}">
                <h3 class="menu-cat-name" id="cat-{{ c.id }}">{{ c.name }}</h3>
                {%- if c.serving %}
                <p class="menu-cat-sizing menu-cat-serving">{{ c.serving }}</p>
                {%- endif %}
                {%- if c.note %}
                <p class="menu-cat-note">{{ c.note }}</p>
                {%- endif %}
                {%- if c.sizing %}
                <p class="menu-cat-sizing">
                    {%- for s in c.sizing %}{{ s.size }} {{ s.price | money }}{% if not loop.last %} &middot; {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
                </p>
                {%- endif %}

                {%- if c.items %}
                <ul class="menu-items" role="list">
                    {%- for item in c.items %}
                    {#- data-diet / data-diet-request are the filter's input,
                        and they carry the same gate as the marks: an
                        unconfirmed classification sitting in view-source is
                        still published, even with nothing drawn on screen. -#}
                    <li class="menu-item"
                        {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed %}
                        {%- if item.diet %} data-diet="{{ item.diet | join(" ") }}"{% endif %}
                        {%- if item.dietOnRequest %} data-diet-request="{{ item.dietOnRequest | join(" ") }}"{% endif %}
                        {%- endif %}>
                        <div class="menu-item-head">
                            <span class="menu-item-name">{{ item.name }}{% if menu.dietsConfirmed %}{{ dietMarks(item, menu.diets) }}{% endif %}</span>
                            <span class="menu-item-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                            {%- if item.price %}
                            <span class="menu-item-price">{{ item.price | money }}</span>
                            {%- elif item.prices %}
                            <span class="menu-item-price">
                                {%- for pr in item.prices %}{{ pr | money }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
                            </span>
                            {%- elif item.variants %}
                            <span class="menu-item-price">
                                {%- for v in item.variants %}{{ v.size }} {{ v.price | money }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
                            </span>
                            {%- endif %}
                        </div>
                        {%- if item.description %}
                        <p class="menu-item-desc">{{ item.description }}</p>
                        {%- endif %}
                    </li>
                    {%- endfor %}
                </ul>
                {%- endif %}

                {#- A build-your-own category — see _note_picks in menu.json.
                    Columns of options under the one category price, instead of
                    a priced row per combination. No diet marks and no filter
                    hooks here: the filter works on .menu-item, and a pick is
                    not an item you can order on its own. -#}
                {%- if c.picks %}
                <div class="menu-picks">
                    {%- for p in c.picks %}
                    <div class="menu-pick">
                        <p class="menu-pick-label">{{ p.label }}</p>
                        <ul class="menu-pick-list" role="list">
                            {%- for o in p.options %}
                            <li class="menu-pick-option">{{ o }}</li>
                            {%- endfor %}
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    {%- endfor %}
                </div>
                {%- endif %}
            </section>
            {%- endfor %}
        </div>

        {#- The tri-fold's own PDF, the same file /studio/downloads/ serves.
            It is built by `npm run build` (the export half), NOT by
            `npm start` — so this link 404s on a dev server until a full
            build has run once. Production always has it, because _site/ is
            what gets uploaded and the build always exports.

            Not a `download` attribute: on a phone that forces a save
            instead of opening the viewer, and most people tapping this want
            to look at it, not file it. -#}
        <p class="menu-print">
            <a href="/studio/downloads/menu-trifold.pdf" class="btn btn-outline">Print this menu (PDF)</a>
            <span class="menu-print-hint">Two sides. Print double-sided, then fold in thirds.</span>
        </p>

        {#- The same menu, set large. Offered as its own link rather than
            hidden behind the tri-fold, because somebody who needs it is
            exactly the person least likely to go hunting: it says what it is
            and what it costs (three sheets) on the face of the link. Built
            by the same `npm run build` export half as the tri-fold above, so
            it has the same dev-server caveat — it 404s until a full build
            has run once. -#}
        <p class="menu-print">
            <a href="/studio/downloads/menu-large-print.pdf" class="btn btn-outline">Print this menu in large type (PDF)</a>
            <span class="menu-print-hint">Three pages, plain paper. Nothing to fold.</span>
        </p>

        <p class="menu-updated">Menu last updated {{ menu._updated }}.</p>
    </div>
</section>

{#- ── Dietary key ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    Below the food, so the items are the first thing on screen. The key is
    plain HTML and is the whole feature with JavaScript off: the marks are
    already on the items, so a reader can decode them here and scroll back.
    The script reads its codes and labels out of this key, so it must stay
    rendered whenever the filter mount above is. -#}
<section class="section section--alt menu-diet" aria-labelledby="diet-heading">
    <div class="container">
        <h2 id="diet-heading" class="menu-diet-heading">Eating gluten free, vegetarian or low sodium?</h2>

        {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed %}
        <dl class="diet-key">
            {%- for d in menu.diets %}
            <div class="diet-key-row">
                <dt><span class="diet-mark">{{ d.code }}</span></dt>
                <dd>{{ d.name }}</dd>
            </div>
            {%- endfor %}
            <div class="diet-key-row">
                <dt><span class="diet-mark">*</span></dt>
                <dd>We can make it that way. Just ask</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
        {%- else %}
        {#- The unconfirmed state. It still answers the question in the
            heading — just with a phone number instead of a badge, which is
            the honest answer until the kitchen has been through the list.
            Setting `dietsConfirmed` in menu.json swaps this for the key,
            the filter above the list, and the marks on every item. -#}
        <p class="diet-pending">
            We are still working out which items are gluten free, vegetarian and low sodium. We would rather tell you nothing than tell you wrong.
            <a href="tel:{{ site.phoneHref }}">Call us at {{ site.phone }}</a> and we will talk you through what the kitchen can make for you.
        </p>
        {%- endif %}

        <p class="diet-caveat">{{ menu.dietNote }}</p>
    </div>
</section>

{#- Plain ground, not --alt: the dietary section above now carries the alt
    ground, and two alt sections back to back read as one. -#}
<section class="section menu-cta" aria-labelledby="menu-cta-heading">
    <div class="container">
        <h2 id="menu-cta-heading">Feeding a crowd?</h2>
        <p class="menu-cta-copy">Trays, box lunches and full-service catering for offices, churches, funerals and family gatherings, built around your date and your guests&rsquo; needs.</p>
        <p class="menu-cta-actions">
            <a href="/catering" class="btn btn-primary">Catering &amp; booking</a>
            <a href="tel:{{ site.phoneHref }}" class="btn btn-outline">Call {{ site.phone }}</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<script>
/* Dietary filter — progressive enhancement.
 *
 * The control is built here rather than written into the template so the
 * page never ships a button that does nothing. Codes and labels are read
 * back out of the rendered key, so menu.json stays the single source: add
 * a diet there and a chip appears here on the next build, no JS change.
 *
 * Selections are ANDed, not ORed. Somebody who taps both "Gluten free" and
 * "Vegetarian" needs an item that is both, and showing the union would
 * hand them a list of things they cannot eat — the one failure mode this
 * feature must not have.
 *
 * On-request marks DO match. "We can make it without the meat" is a real
 * answer to "what can I eat here", and the asterisk on the item says which
 * kind of answer it is.
 */
(function () {
    var mount = document.querySelector('[data-diet-filter]');
    var result = document.querySelector('[data-menu-result]');
    if (!mount || !result) return;

    var items = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.menu-item'));
    var cats = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('[data-menu-cat]'));

    // The trailing "*" row of the key is the on-request note, not a diet.
    var diets = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.diet-key-row'))
        .map(function (row) {
            return {
                code: row.querySelector('.diet-mark').textContent.trim(),
                name: row.querySelector('dd').textContent.trim()
            };
        })
        .filter(function (d) { return d.code !== '*'; });
    if (!diets.length || !items.length) return;

    var active = [];

    function codes(el, attr) {
        var v = el.getAttribute(attr);
        return v ? v.split(' ') : [];
    }

    function apply() {
        var shown = 0;
        items.forEach(function (el) {
            var has = codes(el, 'data-diet').concat(codes(el, 'data-diet-request'));
            var ok = active.every(function (code) { return has.indexOf(code) !== -1; });
            el.hidden = !ok;
            if (ok) shown++;
        });

        // A category with every item filtered out is a heading over nothing.
        cats.forEach(function (cat) {
            cat.hidden = !Array.prototype.slice.call(cat.querySelectorAll('.menu-item'))
                .some(function (el) { return !el.hidden; });
        });

        if (!active.length) {
            result.hidden = true;
            result.textContent = '';
            return;
        }

        var names = active.map(function (code) {
            var match = diets.filter(function (d) { return d.code === code; })[0];
            return match ? match.name.toLowerCase() : code;
        }).join(' and ');

        result.hidden = false;
        result.textContent = shown
            ? shown + (shown === 1 ? ' item is' : ' items are') + ' marked ' + names + '. An asterisk means we make it that way on request.'
            : 'Nothing is marked ' + names + ' yet. Call us and we will tell you what the kitchen can do.';
    }

    function chip(diet) {
        var button = document.createElement('button');
        button.type = 'button';
        button.className = 'diet-chip';
        button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');

        var mark = document.createElement('span');
        mark.className = 'diet-mark';
        mark.textContent = diet.code;
        button.appendChild(mark);
        button.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' ' + diet.name));

        button.addEventListener('click', function () {
            var i = active.indexOf(diet.code);
            var on = i === -1;
            if (on) { active.push(diet.code); } else { active.splice(i, 1); }
            button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', on ? 'true' : 'false');
            button.classList.toggle('is-on', on);
            clear.hidden = active.length === 0;
            apply();
        });
        return button;
    }

    var label = document.createElement('p');
    label.className = 'diet-filter-label';
    label.id = 'diet-filter-label';
    label.textContent = 'Show me only:';

    var group = document.createElement('div');
    group.className = 'diet-chips';
    group.setAttribute('role', 'group');
    group.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', 'diet-filter-label');
    diets.forEach(function (diet) { group.appendChild(chip(diet)); });

    var clear = document.createElement('button');
    clear.type = 'button';
    clear.className = 'diet-clear';
    clear.textContent = 'Show everything';
    clear.hidden = true;
    clear.addEventListener('click', function () {
        active = [];
        Array.prototype.slice.call(group.querySelectorAll('.diet-chip')).forEach(function (button) {
            button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
            button.classList.remove('is-on');
        });
        clear.hidden = true;
        apply();
    });
    group.appendChild(clear);

    mount.appendChild(label);
    mount.appendChild(group);
    mount.hidden = false;
}());
</script>

{#- ── Structured data ─────────────────────────────────────────
    schema.org Menu, hung off the FoodEstablishment that index.njk already
    declares at #business, so the two are one record rather than two
    unrelated things on the same domain.

    `suitableForDiet` is the machine-readable half of the marks above, and
    the reason menu.json's diets carry a `schema` URL. Only `diet` is
    emitted — never `dietOnRequest`. "We can make it that way" has no
    representation in this vocabulary, and publishing it here would state
    flatly that the item ships gluten-free when it does not.

    Every string goes through `dump` so a quote or an apostrophe in an item
    name cannot break the JSON; `safe` then stops Nunjucks re-escaping the
    quotes dump just added into &quot; inside a <script>. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Menu",
  "@id": "{{ site.url }}/menu#menu",
  "name": "The Café menu",
  "url": "{{ site.url }}/menu",
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "provider": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#business" },
  "hasMenuSection": [
    {%- for c in menu.categories %}
    {
      "@type": "MenuSection",
      "name": {{ c.name | dump | safe }},
      {%- if c.note %}
      "description": {{ c.note | dump | safe }},
      {%- endif %}
      {#- A `picks` category has no priced rows of its own, so its options are
          flattened into MenuItems here — a MenuSection published with an empty
          hasMenuItem is a section Google reads as having nothing in it. The
          names are collected first so `loop.last` keys to the flat list rather
          than to the inner loop, which would trail a comma after every column
          but the last. -#}
      {%- set pickNames = [] %}
      {%- for p in c.picks %}{% for o in p.options %}{% set _ = pickNames.push(o) %}{% endfor %}{% endfor %}
      "hasMenuItem": [
        {%- for item in c.items %}
        {
          "@type": "MenuItem",
          "name": {{ item.name | dump | safe }}
          {%- if item.description %},
          "description": {{ item.description | dump | safe }}
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if item.price %},
          "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "{{ item.price | money | replace("$", "") }}", "priceCurrency": "USD" }
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed and item.diet %},
          {#- Collected first, then printed. Deciding the separator inside the
              diets loop keys `loop.last` to the registry rather than to the
              codes this item actually carries, which trails a comma into the
              JSON any time the last registered diet is not one of them. -#}
          {%- set schemas = [] %}
          {%- for d in menu.diets %}{% if d.code in item.diet %}{% set _ = schemas.push(d.schema) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
          "suitableForDiet": [{% for s in schemas %}"{{ s }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}]
          {%- endif %}
        }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
        {%- endfor %}
        {%- if pickNames.length %}{% if c.items %},{% endif %}
        {%- for o in pickNames %}
        {
          "@type": "MenuItem",
          "name": {{ o | dump | safe }}
        }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
        {%- endfor %}
        {%- endif %}
      ]
    }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
    {%- endfor %}
  ]
}
</script>
